; Deny every operation unless a rule below grants it.
(version 1)
(deny default)

; Hide every other process while permitting self-inspection.
(deny process-info*)
(allow process-info* (target self))

; One block per grant cell, below, each in grant order. A cell is one operation at one scope, and
; the kernel takes the union of every allow — so a path granted read and write reaches two blocks
; and needs no block of its own.

; exec at file scope: one exec rule plus a metadata read on the same literal, so a PATH search can
; stat the candidate without reading its bytes.
{{EXEC_FILE}}
(allow process-fork)

; An interactive Agent needs ioctl on an inherited macOS PTY slave, and cannot create a new PTY.
(allow file-ioctl (regex #"^/dev/ttys[0-9]+$"))

; The inherited standard streams: one metadata rule per regular file behind descriptors 0, 1, and 2
; at apply time, on that name while a regular file holds it, so `fstat` on a stream the operator
; redirected to a file no grant names answers instead of refusing. No data and no write travels with
; it, a path the floor refuses gets none, and a refusal below still wins.
{{STANDARD_STREAMS}}

; read at file scope: one whole file, and nothing beside it. The proxy trust bundle is the usual
; one, and this block names no role — whichever files composition granted are the files here.
{{READ_FILE}}

; read at dir scope: present, listable, and holding nothing readable. Metadata resolves the path,
; and on a directory the data operation is enumeration.
{{READ_DIR}}

; read at root scope: the subtree, plus the ancestor metadata a lookup needs to reach it.
{{READ_ROOT}}

; metadata at dir scope: this directory exists, and it is not enumerable.
{{METADATA_DIR}}

; metadata at root scope: every path in the subtree can be stat'd, and none of them read.
{{METADATA_ROOT}}

; write at file scope: the contents of one file, and nothing else. The cell names the one leaf it
; needs, so the BSD file flags, the file's own identity, its access-control list, its permission bits
; and its owner are absent rather than subtracted, and the default deny is what refuses each. A last
; deny takes the executable mapping off it, so the Agent cannot load as code a file it can rewrite.
; The one grant of this shape today is a device the operator owns rather than the box.
{{WRITE_FILE}}

; write at root scope: the subtree's contents are the Agent's, and the root's own identity is not —
; the two identity denies fix it, because a subpath rule covers the root itself and a symlink at that
; path would otherwise succeed. The cell names six leaves: the contents, both halves of an entry's
; identity, the extended attributes, the permission bits, and the timestamps. The BSD file flags, the
; access-control list and the owner are absent, so the default deny refuses each; nothing ordinary
; sets any of the three, and a flag or a list the Agent stored would refuse the operator too and leave
; the box undeletable. The permission bits stay granted because a workload tightens a file it created
; in its own tree. A last deny takes the executable mapping off the whole subtree, so no file the
; Agent can rewrite loads as code. A read-only root keeps its own mapping authority, which is where a
; runtime library comes from.
{{WRITE_ROOT}}

; write protection: subtract every write and hard-link operation from an opened authority object.
{{WRITE_PROTECTION}}

; leaf overlay (empty for the agent box): additive leaf-only broad-exec rules, placed AFTER the write
; cells so an executable-mapping allow overrides their deny, and BEFORE the refused/existence denies
; below so a refused tree stays non-executable. Filled by the broad-exec branch in seatbelt.rs.
{{LEAF_OVERLAY}}

; Seven names and no more. Native Rust startup needs the page size, and a runtime that sizes
; a thread pool needs the processor count. The other five are what the uname interface reads.
; A runtime asks for all of them before it runs a line of the workload's own code. Each names
; a public property of the host, and none is a secret.
(allow sysctl-read
    (sysctl-name "hw.machine")
    (sysctl-name "hw.ncpu")
    (sysctl-name "hw.pagesize_compat")
    (sysctl-name "kern.hostname")
    (sysctl-name "kern.osrelease")
    (sysctl-name "kern.ostype")
    (sysctl-name "kern.version"))

{{RUNTIME_SERVICES}}
; TCP egress uses the composition-owned ports; Unix sockets use write roots or exact connect grants.
(deny network*)

; Pathname sockets: bind, inbound, and outbound on write roots; outbound on each connect file.
{{CONNECT_FILE}}

; the composition's own localhost ports, in grant order. The first is the proxy.
{{CONNECT_PORTS}}

; The existence test, refused across the operator's home. Denying by default leaves it answerable,
; and denying this one operation is what makes an absent path and a refused path indistinguishable.
; Three parts in one block, and their order decides the outcome under last-match-wins: the home
; first, then the operation allowed back on each granted path and on the ancestor chain a lookup
; walks, then every credential store denied last. A machine-wide deny is unavailable, because it
; refuses the workload's own exec.
{{DENY_EXISTENCE}}

; Refused trees remain closed to content reads, writes, and execution.
{{DENY_REFUSED}}

; Metadata and existence lookup for identity-checked files and their ancestors.
{{IDENTITY_LOOKUP}}

; View-changing and node-creating operations, each named in full and placed last so no cell above and
; no future overlay re-grants one. Device nodes; mounting, remounting and unmounting; the change of
; filesystem root; every volume-snapshot operation, mounting one included; and the grafting of a
; filesystem image into the namespace. Naming each in full is required rather than tidy: the
; version-1 preamble grants several of these unconditionally, the default deny does not reach them,
; and a wildcard does not take back an operation that preamble granted — only the exact name does.
; The two grafting operations are absent from the operation table before macOS 26, and an absent name
; refuses the whole profile at compile time. So each of the two states a test of its own name first,
; and the refusal applies on every host that carries the operation. A host without the name renders
; neither line, which the crate guidance records as a stated cost.
(deny file-mknod)
(deny file-mount)
(deny file-mount-update)
(deny file-unmount)
(deny file-chroot)
(deny fs-snapshot-create)
(deny fs-snapshot-delete)
(deny fs-snapshot-mount)
(deny fs-snapshot-revert)
(if (defined? 'file-graft) (deny file-graft))
(if (defined? 'file-ungraft) (deny file-ungraft))
